{"context": "A research team was mapping out which areas of AI safety work could be pursued as for-profit ventures. They observed that commercial pressure to show a business case can pull organizations toward adjacent work with clearer revenue, and that some areas of safety lack an obvious product. The areas under consideration ranged from productizable technical work to theory-oriented research and AI governance.\n\nQuestion: Which areas of AI safety work, if any, should the team treat as unsuitable for for-profit ventures?", "options": ["Treat most governance work as unsuitable for for-profit ventures and keep it in non-profits or small insulated teams inside larger organizations.", "Treat safety work as unsuited to for-profit ventures absent a concrete buyer whose purchase itself raises safety, defaulting to non-profit funding."], "principles": [], "persona": "An AI safety researcher or technical forecaster who prizes epistemic rigor, precise conceptual distinctions, and methodological soundness. They insist on clear definitional boundaries—scheming vs. reward hacking, distinct task capabilities vs. single capability estimates—over tidy collapsible taxonomies. They favor theory-first reasoning (coverage claims need defined criteria) paired with concrete, mechanism-focused empirical evidence over speculative arguments. They are scope-disciplined: they prefer tractable, well-founded analyses over ambitious extensions built on noisy inputs, and conservative, hedged forecasts that flag model limitations over confident headline numbers. On alignment risk, they take deceptive alignment seriously as an incentive-structure concern while remaining cautious about unproven claims, favoring layered defenses (control, interpretability, oversight), mechanism-aware measurement, and mitigations that don't merely train models to hide the measured signal. They distrust lab-to-deployment extrapolation, preferring real-world data even if noisy. They are skeptical of low-evidence-value experiments with confounded interpretations, preferring controlled baselines, non-IID generalization tests, and diagnostics that cleanly distinguish hypotheses. They value conceptual generality (architecture-agnostic interpretability, universal learning dynamics) and calm, accessible public communication.", "past_choices": []}
